# ============================ POS 6 ============================
LESSONS_M6 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The extended counters", "10", "<p>Beyond the Sales Console, the sidebar carries a set of consoles that look like customer services: Lay-By, Staff Purchase, Airtime Sales, Sales History, Customer Desk, and Inventory Ops. This module covers each one. The module's law: <b>every extended counter is a financial arrangement, and each is run with the controls its money deserves.</b></p><p><b>What each console really handles.</b> Look past the service and name the money:</p><p>• <b>Lay-By</b> holds a customer's deposit against reserved goods. The business is holding the customer's money (owed back if the arrangement fails) <b>and</b> the customer's goods (out of the sellable pool). Two exposures in one arrangement.<br>• <b>Staff Purchase</b> is employee credit — the business lending against future wages, with a limit, a running balance, and repayment through payroll.<br>• <b>Airtime</b> is prepaid stock whose entire value is a code. It is the closest thing the counter sells to cash itself.<br>• <b>Customer Desk</b> is the customer's financial record — balances, invoices and payments per person.<br>• <b>Inventory Ops</b> is stock moving between locations — requests, transfers, and the receiving check.</p><p><b>Why the naming matters.</b> These counters are exactly where informal shops leak: the lay-by book nobody reconciles, staff purchases sorted out at month end, an airtime float that is about right, a customer file with four spellings of one person. Each of those is a money control skipped because the arrangement looked like a service. ZhiftPOS builds the controls in — deposits computed and taken, limits enforced, codes counted, records merged — and this module shows how each works and what to check.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: creating a lay-by. Chapter 3: managing lay-bys through to completion or termination. Chapter 4: staff purchases and the payroll connection. Chapter 5: airtime sales and the consumed-on-issue rule. Chapter 6: Sales History and the Customer Desk, including duplicate merging. Chapter 7: Inventory Ops, Label Printing and the Message Hub. Chapter 8: Okelewo's rollout of all of them. Chapter 9: review.</p><p><b>One connection to note now.</b> Everything these consoles do posts into the same system as ordinary sales: lay-by instalments appear in the shift close, staff purchase balances sit in a real account and are recovered through payroll, airtime is stocked and counted like inventory, and transfers move real stock. There are no side books — which is the point.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Creating a lay-by", "12", "<p>A lay-by reserves goods against a deposit, with the balance paid over scheduled instalments. Creating one properly takes four steps and four habits.</p><p><b>Procedure.</b></p><p>1. <b>Add the items</b> the customer is reserving, exactly as in a normal sale — including batch and serial entry where the goods are tracked. Reserved goods are still tracked goods.<br>2. <b>Identify the customer by phone number.</b> A lay-by is never anonymous: the arrangement will need follow-up, and the phone number is how the follow-up happens. Read the number back or ring it once at entry.<br>3. <b>Review the total and the deposit.</b> The required deposit is computed from the profile's Lay-By Deposit % (module 2). At Okelewo this is 15%.<br>4. <b>Take the deposit.</b> On payment, the instalment schedule is created from the profile's instalment count and day spacing.</p><p><b>The schedule.</b> Each instalment carries: <b>due date, amount due, amount paid, balance, and status</b> — unpaid, partly paid, paid, or overdue. The schedule is, in effect, a small receivables ledger for this one arrangement, and the statuses are what Chapter 3's management works from.</p><p><b>Four habits that prevent the classic lay-by problems.</b></p><p>• <b>Take the deposit before setting anything aside.</b> A lay-by without its deposit is a free reservation — the customer risks nothing, and abandoned arrangements pile up. The profile computes the minimum; collect it at creation, always.<br>• <b>Print and explain the schedule.</b> The customer should leave holding the dates and amounts. A payment reminder weeks later should be a reminder, not a surprise.<br>• <b>Physically separate reserved goods.</b> A lay-by shelf, away from the sales floor. A reserved item left on the shelf gets sold on a busy Saturday, and the business then owes a customer goods it no longer has.<br>• <b>Verify the phone number at entry.</b> Everything in Chapter 3 — the overdue call, the termination notice — depends on the number being real.</p><p><b>Decide the forfeiture policy before the first dispute.</b> What happens to the deposit if the customer abandons the lay-by? Whatever the answer, decide it as policy, print it on the schedule, and say it at creation. A forfeiture rule announced at the start generates no argument later; one invented during a dispute generates nothing else. (Okelewo's version, and how it played out, is in Chapter 8.)</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Items in, phone verified, deposit computed and taken, schedule created, printed and explained, goods physically set aside, forfeiture terms known. A lay-by created this way is easy to manage; every shortcut here becomes a problem in Chapter 3.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Managing lay-bys", "12", "<p>Every lay-by ends one of two ways — completed or terminated. The console manages both, and the long middle between.</p><p><b>The Open Lay-Bys panel.</b> All outstanding lay-bys, each with its schedule and statuses. Read it as an ageing list: the <b>overdue</b> instalments are the work. Check the panel weekly (it is on module 8's calendar), and follow up overdue instalments by phone from the recorded number. A lay-by left silent for months becomes the classic mess: goods held for someone unreachable, a deposit nobody can match to a person, and stock in limbo.</p><p><b>Taking an instalment: Make Payment.</b></p><p>1. Open the lay-by and choose Make Payment.<br>2. Enter the amount and choose the payment method.<br>3. The schedule updates — instalments move to paid or partly paid.</p><p>Notes: payments in any workable pattern are fine — two instalments at once, or half of one; that is what partly paid exists for. Each payment lands on its payment method and is included in the shift close — <b>lay-by activity is summarised in the Close Shift window</b>, so these takings are counted with everything else. Print the updated schedule for the customer each time.</p><p><b>Completion.</b> When the final instalment is paid, the goods are released to the customer. The reservation becomes a completed sale, and the shelf clears.</p><p><b>Termination.</b> Terminate cancels the lay-by. The procedure has controls built in:</p><p>1. Choose Terminate on the lay-by.<br>2. Select the refund method for what the customer has paid (subject to the forfeiture policy, and routed like any refund — store credit where cash refunds are off).<br>3. <b>A manager's email and password are required.</b> Termination is always an authorised event: money is going backwards and goods are being released from a customer arrangement, and cancelling-and-pocketing is the lay-by's classic fraud. The manager step is the control.<br>4. On completion, <b>the goods return to stock</b> — the reservation is released and the inventory is correct again.</p><p>The terminated lay-by's record survives: who reserved, what was paid, who authorised the end. Nothing about a termination is deleted.</p><p><b>The three numbers a healthy lay-by book shows.</b> At any moment, the console should be able to answer: total deposits and instalments held, total balances still due, and which goods are reserved. All three reconcile against reality — the money against the accounts, the goods against the lay-by shelf. Module 8's routines include exactly that check.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Staff purchases", "12", "<p>Staff Purchase lets employees buy at a staff discount and repay through payroll. It is employee credit with the controls built in — a limit, a visible balance, automatic recovery — and this chapter covers the flow and the setup that makes it real.</p><p><b>The purchase, step by step.</b></p><p>1. Open the Staff Purchase console and enter the <b>employee identifier</b>.<br>2. The screen shows the employee's credit position: <b>purchase limit, current exposure, remaining balance, and estimated monthly repayment</b>. The employee sees their own position before anything is added.<br>3. Add the items. The <b>staff discount applies automatically to every line</b> — the percentage from the profile (which takes precedence over any company-wide staff discount at this counter).<br>4. Complete. Two things happen: the purchase is <b>booked to the employee purchase account</b>, and it is <b>scheduled for recovery through payroll</b>.</p><p><b>The hard limit.</b> A purchase that would take the employee past their limit is <b>refused</b>. There is no override at the till, by design: the limit is set on the employee's record by whoever manages employment terms, and that is where any exception belongs. The refusal is rarely contested in practice, because the screen showed the arithmetic before the attempt.</p><p><b>The wiring that must exist.</b> Three records make the feature work end to end — confirm all three before enabling it:</p><p>1. A <b>purchase limit</b> on each participating employee's record.<br>2. The <b>employee purchase account</b> named in the company-wide settings — where outstanding balances sit, visible to finance per person.<br>3. The <b>salary component</b> named in the company-wide settings — the payroll deduction that recovers the balances.</p><p>Then prove the chain once: one real purchase, through the account, into a payroll run, recovered by the component. A half-wired setup — purchases booking but recovery never configured — is an interest-free loan book growing quietly under a staff-benefit label. The one-time test prevents it.</p><p><b>The Open Staff Purchases panel.</b> Lists outstanding balances, searchable by staff name or reference, each showing total and remaining balance. Review it on the same weekly rhythm as the lay-by panel.</p><p><b>Take Payment.</b> Records a <b>direct repayment outside payroll</b> — an employee clearing some or all of a balance in cash before the payroll run. The payment lands on its method, the exposure reduces, and the payroll recovery adjusts to the remainder.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Identifier → position shown → discount automatic → limit enforced with no till override → booked to the account → recovered by payroll (or by Take Payment). Wire all three records and test the chain before the first purchase.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Airtime sales", "12", "<p>The Airtime console sells mobile recharge vouchers. The product is a code: reveal it and it is spent. That one fact drives every rule in this chapter.</p><p><b>The sale, in three steps.</b></p><p>1. <b>Select Carrier</b> — choose the network.<br>2. <b>Choose Amount</b> — pick a denomination and set the quantity. Availability is shown, and <b>the sale is capped at what is in stock</b>: airtime is inventory, and the console will not sell codes it does not hold.<br>3. <b>Complete Payment</b> — choose the method and finish.</p><p>Each voucher then prints on <b>its own slip with its recharge code</b> — one slip per code.</p><p><b>The consumed-on-issue rule.</b> Memorise this: <b>an airtime code is consumed the moment it is issued.</b> When the sale completes, that code is revealed from stock and is spent — whether or not the paper reached the customer's hand. Two instructions follow:</p><p>• <b>Print before the customer leaves.</b> The slip is the product.<br>• <b>If the printer fails, reprint from the invoice — never sell again.</b> Vouchers can be reprinted by looking up the invoice number: the same code is recovered, on the same sale, with no second consumption. Ringing a new sale instead consumes a <b>second</b> code against one payment — one code in the customer's hand, one lost (or worse, in a pocket). The reflex to train: printer fails → note the invoice number → reprint. Never resell.</p><p><b>Airtime as stock.</b> Treat the codes like the inventory they are:</p><p>• The availability display is the stock count — watch it, and reorder from the sales figures before running out. (At Okelewo, recharge vouchers turned out to be the flagship's top-selling item — an airtime stockout there is a revenue event.)<br>• The reconciliation is simple arithmetic: <b>codes received − codes issued = codes held.</b> It is as countable as the cash drawer, and it goes on the same routine: a count that does not reconcile has a story that needs finding.</p><p><b>Reprints and disputes.</b> Any airtime voucher can be reprinted from its invoice. A customer returning with a code they say did not work is handled from the record: the invoice shows the code that was issued, when, and on which sale — the conversation starts from facts, not memory.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Carrier → amount and quantity (stock-capped) → payment → one slip per code. The code is consumed at issue: print before the customer leaves, reprint by invoice on failure, never resell. Count received − issued = held on the regular routine.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Sales History and the Customer Desk", "13", "<p>Two lookup consoles turn the counter's records into answers: Sales History for transactions, the Customer Desk for people.</p><p><b>Sales History.</b> Recent transactions, with tools to find any of them:</p><p>• <b>Filter</b> by date range; <b>search</b> by reference or customer name; <b>narrow</b> to voucher activity, a single cashier, or a single shift.<br>• Returns are marked with a <b>Return label</b>, so they are never mistaken for sales.<br>• Any entry opens to its lines and recorded payments, and the receipt can be reprinted from there.</p><p>The everyday use case — <b>the lost receipt</b>: search by the customer's name, or any fragment of the reference. Offline sales are found by their local reference just as posted sales are found by their invoice number. A lost receipt is a one-minute lookup, not a dead end.</p><p>Note for supervisors: the cashier and shift filters make this console the starting point for reviewing any specific person or period — module 8 uses them.</p><p><b>The Customer Desk.</b> One customer's full picture, in three views:</p><p>• <b>Details</b> — contact information, price list, credit limit, payment terms and loyalty points, plus the statistics: <b>outstanding balance, total invoiced, total paid, total orders, average order value, purchase frequency, last purchase, and return rate</b>. Read the return rate: a customer returning a third of what they buy is a pattern worth understanding, whatever the explanation turns out to be.<br>• <b>Invoices</b> — everything bought, filterable by status and date, searchable by number, printable.<br>• <b>Payments</b> — receipts with reference, date and amount.</p><p><b>Edit Customer</b> updates the name, mobile, email and loyalty card from the same screen.</p><p><b>Duplicate customers and merging.</b> Two protections keep one person as one record:</p><p>• <b>Automatic merge at creation</b> (on by default): a new customer whose <b>mobile number matches</b> an existing record merges into it immediately. Leave this on unless customers genuinely share phone numbers.<br>• <b>The merge tool</b>, for duplicates that slip through: it scans for customers sharing a mobile or email, you choose the surviving record, and the history merges into it. The merge is <b>reversible</b> — a snapshot is kept, and cancelling the merge restores both records.</p><p><b>Run the duplicate scan monthly.</b> The practical reason: loyalty depends on it. A customer split across two records has their points split too — half their history on each — and they will notice. The desk's statistics also assume one person per record; duplicates quietly corrupt every figure on the Details view.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Sales History: filter, search, Return labels, reprints — the lost receipt answered in a minute. Customer Desk: details with statistics (watch the return rate), invoices, payments, edit. Duplicates: mobile-match auto-merge at creation, the reversible merge tool monthly.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Inventory Ops, Label Printing, and the Message Hub", "13", "<p>Three consoles connect the counter to stock: what is held, what is requested, what is moving — plus the labels for the shelf and the hub that carries the traffic.</p><p><b>The Inventory tab.</b> Stock as this terminal knows it:</p><p>• Filter by <b>warehouse</b> (where the profile permits viewing other warehouses — the is-it-at-the-other-branch question answered from the chair), by item group, or by free search on code, name or brand.<br>• Counters summarise items <b>in stock, low on stock, and out of stock</b>.<br>• A <b>synchronising marker</b> appears while figures refresh — the number on screen is the local copy's number, and the marker shows when it is being updated.</p><p><b>Material Requests — asking another location for stock.</b></p><p>1. Choose New Request. Confirm the requesting warehouse and select the <b>source</b> warehouse.<br>2. Set a <b>priority</b>: low, medium, high or urgent.<br>3. Add each item and quantity, plus a <b>note</b> explaining what is needed and why.<br>4. Submit.</p><p>Both directions are visible: requests you have raised <b>and</b> requests raised against your branch, each opening to its full item list and status. The request replaces the phone call and the chat message with a document the other branch can act on and everyone can see.</p><p><b>Stock Transfers — goods actually moving.</b> A transfer is raised at the sending side and <b>received</b> at yours. The receiving rule is the one to memorise: <b>confirm the quantities as you accept them.</b> If twelve were sent and eleven arrived, record eleven. A short delivery confirmed at receiving creates the discrepancy record at the boundary between branches, where it can be investigated cheaply. A short delivery waved through at the paper quantity disappears into your branch's stock figures and resurfaces months later as an unexplainable count variance. Ten seconds of counting at the door, or a mystery in December — that is the trade.</p><p><b>Label Printing.</b> Shelf and product labels, printed from the till:</p><p>• Add items singly by search, or <b>in bulk</b> — an entire item group, or all the lines from a goods receipt or stock entry (a new delivery labelled without retyping).<br>• Set the label count per row, choose the template (module 3's proven presets), preview, print.<br>• <b>Works offline</b> — the shelf can be priced through an outage.</p><p><b>The Message Hub.</b> The counter's inbox: notices, transfer alerts and requests, filed like mail — unread, starred, transfers, requests, system, alerts, archived — searchable, with important notices pinned and the unread count on the top-bar envelope. The operational point: <b>transfer and request messages carry their underlying details and can be acted on directly from the hub</b>. Keep the counter's operational traffic here, in one visible stream, rather than scattered across personal phones.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: Okelewo's extended counters", "13", "<p>Okelewo enables the extended counters in its second quarter, demand first. The events worth learning from:</p><p><b>Lay-by (Chapters 2-3).</b> Launched first — customers had been asking, especially for big-ticket items. The creation habits hold from day one: 15% deposits taken at creation, schedules printed and explained, a lay-by shelf established behind the flagship counter, phone numbers read back at entry. The forfeiture policy is decided <b>before launch</b> and printed on every schedule.</p><p>The test arrives on schedule: one lay-by goes two instalments overdue. The weekly panel check flags it; two calls to the recorded number go unanswered; the termination runs by the book — manager credentials at the gate, the refundable portion routed to store credit (cash refunds are still off), the goods back to stock the same hour, the record intact. The retained portion follows the printed policy, and there is no argument, because <b>the schedule said what the schedule said</b> from the day it was created.</p><p><b>Staff purchase (Chapter 4).</b> Enabled at the flagship only, and only after the full-chain test: one real purchase, through the employee purchase account, into a payroll run, recovered by the salary component — proven before the second employee bought anything. The quarter stays uneventful, which is the point: limits visible to their holders, one over-limit attempt refused without argument (the screen had shown the arithmetic), one Take Payment repayment ahead of payroll, and finance reading the account per person at month end. The old informal habit — staff goods sorted out at month end from memory — simply stops, because the ledger replaced it.</p><p><b>Airtime (Chapter 5).</b> The surprise of the quarter: recharge vouchers are the flagship's <b>top-selling item</b> — six hundred units of one denomination in a month. Airtime stock is promoted to a managed float: availability checked daily, reorders triggered from the sales report. The consumed-on-issue drill pays off in week nine: a printer jams mid-sale, and the cashier's trained reflex runs — invoice number noted, slip reprinted, same code recovered. The untrained response (ring it again) would have consumed a second code against one payment. The received − issued = held count joins the daily routine and reconciles all quarter.</p><p><b>The record consoles (Chapter 6).</b> A lost-receipt customer is served in ninety seconds from Sales History — name search, an offline-era local reference found, receipt reprinted. The Customer Desk's return-rate figure flags one account at 31%; investigation finds a genuine cause — a sizing problem in one product line — which goes to purchasing as buying feedback. The figure was worth reading either way. The first monthly duplicate scan finds nine pairs; they are merged with survivors chosen — and one merge is <b>reversed</b> the next day when a shared family phone number comes to light. The snapshot restores both records exactly as promised.</p><p><b>Receiving (Chapter 7).</b> The first short delivery is caught at the flagship door: twelve sent from the Distribution Centre, eleven received, eleven <b>confirmed</b>. The discrepancy record points the investigation at the sending warehouse, where a pick error is found within the week. Under the old routine, the missing unit would have dissolved into the flagship's stock figures and reappeared in December as unexplained shrinkage.</p><p><b>The MD's quarter note:</b> <i>the side counters turned out to be money wearing disguises. We run them with the same controls now, and the month-end sorting-out meetings have nothing left to sort.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Review & the sixth law", "11", "<p>Check yourself against each section before the quiz.</p><p><b>The consoles named (Chapter 1).</b> Lay-By = a deposit held against reserved goods (two exposures). Staff Purchase = employee credit recovered through payroll. Airtime = prepaid stock whose value is the code. Customer Desk = the customer's financial record. Inventory Ops = stock in motion. No side books — everything posts into the same system.</p><p><b>Creating a lay-by (Chapter 2).</b> Items (with batches/serials), customer by verified phone number, deposit computed from the profile percentage and <b>taken</b>, schedule created from the count and spacing. Print and explain the schedule; separate the goods physically; decide and print the forfeiture policy before the first dispute. Each instalment: due date, amounts, balance, status (unpaid / partly paid / paid / overdue).</p><p><b>Managing lay-bys (Chapter 3).</b> Check the Open Lay-Bys panel weekly; the overdue lines are the work. Make Payment updates the schedule, lands on its method, and is included in the shift close. Completion releases the goods. Termination requires a <b>manager's email and password</b>, routes the refund per policy, returns the goods to stock, and keeps the full record.</p><p><b>Staff purchases (Chapter 4).</b> The screen shows limit, exposure, remaining balance and estimated repayment before anything is added. The discount applies automatically. Over-limit purchases are refused with no till override — the exception belongs with the employee record's owner. Wiring: the employee limit, the purchase account, the salary component — and one full-chain test before launch. Take Payment records direct repayment outside payroll.</p><p><b>Airtime (Chapter 5).</b> Carrier → denomination and quantity (capped at stock) → payment → one slip per code. The code is consumed the moment it is issued: print before the customer leaves; on printer failure, reprint from the invoice number — never sell again. Count: received − issued = held.</p><p><b>The record consoles (Chapter 6).</b> Sales History: filters by date, reference, customer, voucher, cashier, shift; Return labels; reprints; the lost receipt found by either reference. Customer Desk: details with statistics (watch the return rate), invoices, payments, edit. Duplicates: mobile-match auto-merge at creation; the monthly merge tool, reversible by snapshot; loyalty depends on one person, one record.</p><p><b>Stock consoles (Chapter 7).</b> Inventory tab: warehouse filter where permitted, the three stock counters, the synchronising marker. Material requests: source, priority, items, note — both directions visible. Transfers: <b>confirm quantities at receiving</b> — record the short at the boundary instead of absorbing it. Label Printing: bulk from a goods receipt, works offline. Message Hub: one stream, actionable transfer and request messages.</p><p><b>The quarter (Chapter 8).</b> The printed forfeiture policy ending arguments before they start; the full-chain staff purchase test; the printer-jam reprint reflex; the 31% return rate that turned out to be buying feedback; the reversed merge; the short delivery caught at the door.</p><p><b>The sixth law: every extended counter is a financial arrangement, run with the controls its money deserves.</b> Module 7 covers the gift voucher programme end to end.</p>"),
]

